{"id": 36, "ts": 1780576975.2956336, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I concur with Sarah\u2019s provisional recommendation of Candidate\u202fC. The combined evidence shows C is 100\u202f% reliable, stays calm in a crisis, makes correct decisions quickly, and understands complicated technology\u2014covering operational reliability, stress resilience, decision quality, and technical competence. While we lack concrete incidents of C\u2019s egocentric style harming crew cooperation, the absence of such evidence means the concern remains speculative.\nIn contrast, the recent inquiry about Candidate\u202fD yielded no examples of teamwork under high\u2011stress conditions, leaving a critical gap in our assessment of his crew\u2011cooperation and stress\u2011resilience, especially given his noted hot temper. Without proof that D can maintain a constructive cockpit environment during emergencies, his strong problem\u2011solving and responsibility traits cannot offset this uncertainty.\nSimilarly, Candidate\u202fB\u2019s weak memory for numbers raises a potential attention\u2011to\u2011detail risk, yet we have no direct observations of how this impacts operational accuracy. Until we see evidence that B\u2019s memory limitation translates into safety\u2011relevant errors, his conscientiousness and stress handling remain strong but not definitively superior to C\u2019s proven reliability.\nGiven the current evidence hierarchy\u2014C\u2019s strengths are well\u2011substantiated across core safety criteria, while D and B each have unresolved, safety\u2011critical gaps\u2014I maintain my vote for Candidate\u202fC."}
